\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Torus cohomology and exact cochain multiplicities}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{For the [dual cellular complex](fdip-00NL) and every
#{N\ge2}, including composite #{N},
##{H^j(K^*;A)\cong A^{\binom dj}\qquad(0\le j\le d).}
Writing #{|Z^j(K^*;A)|=N^{z_j}}, the integers #{z_j}
are determined by the actual dual cell counts:
##{z_0=1,\qquad
 z_{j+1}=n_j^*-z_j+\binom d{j+1}\quad(0\le j<d).}
Consequently #{|Z^{d-3}|=N} for #{d=3}, and
#{|Z^{d-3}|=N^{n_0^*+3}} for #{d=4}.
These cardinalities are units in the [weight ring](fdip-00NI).}

\proof{
\p{Give each circle one vertex and one oriented edge.
Its integral cellular boundary is zero. The product CW
structure on #{T^d} has #{\binom dj} cells in degree #{j};
the graded product boundary formula makes every differential
zero. This formula is
\citet{Proposition 3B.1, pp. 269–271}{hatcher2002algebraic}.
Applying #{\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb Z}(-,A)} directly
gives cohomology #{A^{\binom dj}}. Cellular cohomology with
any abelian coefficient group agrees with singular cohomology
by \citet{Theorem 3.5, p. 203}{hatcher2002algebraic}.
Applying that comparison also to #{K^*} proves the claimed
isomorphism for the given cellulation. This compares
cohomology; it asserts no invariance of the weights under
subdivision.}

\p{The dual one-skeleton is connected, so a zero-cocycle is
constant and #{|Z^0|=N}. The exact sequences of finite
additive groups
##{\begin{gathered}
 0\longrightarrow Z^j\longrightarrow C^j
    \overset{\delta}{\longrightarrow}B^{j+1}
    \longrightarrow0,\\
 0\longrightarrow B^{j+1}\longrightarrow Z^{j+1}
    \longrightarrow H^{j+1}\longrightarrow0
\end{gathered}}
give
##{\begin{aligned}
 |B^{j+1}|&=N^{n_j^*}/|Z^j|,\\
 |Z^{j+1}|&=|B^{j+1}|N^{\binom d{j+1}}.
\end{aligned}}
Every fiber of a surjective group homomorphism is a coset
of its kernel, which proves these cardinality products.
Induction gives the displayed recurrence. Since
#{|Z^j|\le|C^j|}, each exponent #{n_j^*-z_j} is
nonnegative. All cardinalities used as denominators are
powers of the unit #{N}. Substitution of #{d=3} and
#{d=4} gives the two stated redundancies.}
}

\p{Mathlib's
\leanref{Submodule.card_eq_card_quotient_mul_card}
supports the finite module cardinality step over a ring.
Neither this step nor the proof above treats arbitrary
submodules over #{\mathbb Z/N} as vector spaces.}
